---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const six = assertion('six-registrations-two-working-faces')
const place = assertion('a-quarry-inside-a-village')
const ford = assertion('the-quarry-and-the-engine-plant')
const inj = assertion('thirty-four-injuries-and-no-death')
const date = assertion('the-registers-oldest-date-is-not-a-date')

const spec = (a: typeof six, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle, labelValues: true },
  )

const sixSpec = spec(six, 'Average employees, both quarries')
const injSpec = spec(inj, 'Reportable injuries per 200,000 hours')
---

<Article slug="two-working-faces">
  <p>
    Under the glacial till that carries this county's corn there is limestone, close enough to the
    surface in two places that it is worth taking out. The federal mine regulator keeps a register
    of everywhere it supervises that work, with coordinates, quarterly hours and every injury, and
    Allen County has six entries in it.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={six} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="six"
    title="Thirty people, give or take ten"
    caption="Average employment across the county's two working quarries. The peak is 2006 and the trough is 2018."
    columns={['Year', 'Employees']}
    rows={six.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="six" set:html={JSON.stringify(sixSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Four of the six are marked abandoned — two Western Ohio Stone quarries in 1984, a Ward Materials
    sand and gravel pit in 1987, and a second on Mayberry Road in 2009. That is a register of
    permits rather than a map of holes: an operation leaves the file when somebody stops filing for
    it, and whatever is at those four places is at them still.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={place} />
  <p>
    The Bluffton one is the surprise. A working limestone quarry sits inside a village's corporate
    limits, which is a fact about zoning and about what a village of four thousand people has been
    willing to live next to for a very long time.
  </p>

  <h2>The ground under the factory</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={ford} />
  <p>
    This corpus already had the other end of that chain. The Recorder's tract book for the north-west
    quarter of section 17 shows DTI Enterprises conveying to Ford Motor Company on 24 August 1955,
    and DTI buying it from the National Lime &amp; Stone Company the June before; National Lime &amp;
    Stone had it from the National Quarries Company in January 1944, and National Quarries from 1917.
    The land that became Bath Township's engine plant came off a quarry company's books — and a mile
    and a half south, the same company is still taking out stone.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={inj} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="inj"
    title="How dangerous the stone is"
    caption="Reportable injuries per 200,000 hours worked, 2000 to 2025. The county's OSHA-reporting establishments run 3.69 on the same arithmetic, its nursing homes 8.38 and its refinery 0.73."
    columns={['Quarry', 'Rate']}
    rows={inj.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(2)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="inj" set:html={JSON.stringify(injSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Thirty-four injuries across twenty-six years and about thirty people is an ordinary rate for this
    county rather than a low one — quarrying here is about as dangerous as the average of everything
    else measured, twice as dangerous as the refinery, and less than half as dangerous as a nursing
    home. Eleven of the thirty-four came from handling materials and eight from a slip or a fall.
  </p>

  <h2>The date that is not a date</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={date} />
  <Gloss>
    Which is why the corpus counts before it cites. One January morning cannot be when a quarter of
    American mining changed hands, and no single row says whether it is that row's real date or the
    file's floor — the value is plausible, well formed, and passes every bounds, type and null check
    there is. It fails only a frequency check, and a frequency check is not something anybody runs on
    a field they are reading one row of. The second and third commonest values tell the same story:
    1 January 1979, and 9 March 1978, which is the day the Act that created this register took
    effect. So a defaulted field is recorded as missing rather than as early.
  </Gloss>
  <p>
    The corpus's own evidence settles it here. The Recorder has National Lime &amp; Stone buying
    ground in this county in January 1944, six years before the register says the company existed in
    it. How long stone has actually been taken at the Lima face is a question for the tract books,
    and it stays open.
  </p>
</Article>
